    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2007,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Erectile dysfunction: verified human evidence from two eligible records. (1) Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-design study at 15 US centers in adults aged 18 years or older with erectile dysfunction; after a 4-week treatment-free run-in, participants received tadalafil 2.5 mg or 5 mg once daily or placebo for 24 weeks. Sample size: not verified in reviewed source. Tadalafil significantly improved the International Index of Erectile Function Erectile Function Domain score and Sexual Encounter Profile diary questions 2 and 3 versus placebo. Adverse events included nasopharyngitis, influenza, viral gastroenteritis, and back pain; discontinuation because of adverse events was 2.1% with placebo, 6.3% with tadalafil 2.5 mg, and 4.1% with tadalafil 5 mg. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. [47]\n\n(2) Integrated analysis of four 12-week trials and one 24-week randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 trial, including 1,112 men with erectile dysfunction (mean age 58 years; range 21–82); 329 had hypertension and 783 did not. Tadalafil doses were 2.5–20 mg taken as needed, maximum once daily. Tadalafil significantly improved International Index of Erectile Function, Sexual Encounter Profile diary, and Global Assessment Question endpoints versus placebo; up to 81% of tadalafil-treated men in the 12-week studies reported improved erections, and the mean percentage of successful intercourse attempts improved to up to 75%. Adverse events were generally mild to moderate and transient; headache, dyspepsia, and back pain were most commonly reported. Key limitation: this record is an integrated analysis rather than a single separately reported trial, and the reviewed source does not verify additional study-level details. [47, 48]",
    "efficacyScore": 4,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 4,
    "safetySummary": "In the 2007 daily-dose study, adverse-event discontinuation was 2.1% with placebo, 6.3% with tadalafil 2.5 mg, and 4.1% with tadalafil 5 mg; common treatment-emergent adverse events were nasopharyngitis, influenza, viral gastroenteritis, and back pain. [47] In the integrated analysis, adverse events were generally mild to moderate and transient; headache, dyspepsia, and back pain were the most commonly reported adverse events. [48]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [46, 47, 48]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [46, 47, 48]",
    "limitations": "The audit reviewed exactly three candidate records. One was a 2003 review and was not counted as a primary human outcome study. The two counted records concern tadalafil for erectile dysfunction; evidence was not transferred to other formulations, analogs, indications, or unreported populations. Study-level details absent from the reviewed summaries are not verified in reviewed sources. [47] [47, 48]",
    "missingFields": "For the 2007 daily-dose randomized study, sample size and a study-specific key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. For the integrated analysis, a study-specific key limitation beyond its integrated-analysis format was not verified in the reviewed source. [47] [47, 48]",
