    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Knee osteoarthritis pain and physical function: (1) Prospective, randomized, double-blind, controlled clinical trial; 49 men and women aged 45–90 years with radiographically confirmed knee osteoarthritis; MSM 1.125 g three times daily for 12 weeks versus placebo at the same dosing frequency. WOMAC physical function and WOMAC total score differed significantly between groups over time, while WOMAC pain, WOMAC stiffness, SF-36 total score, and secondary outcomes were not significantly different; the authors concluded that improvements in pain and physical function were small and their clinical significance remained uncertain. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed summary. Key limitation: the improvements were small and clinical significance was undetermined. [67]\n\n(2) Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled pilot clinical trial; 50 men and women aged 40–76 years with knee osteoarthritis pain; MSM 3 g twice daily (6 g/day total) for 12 weeks versus placebo. MSM significantly decreased WOMAC pain and physical-function impairment and improved activities of daily living on SF-36, but did not notably change WOMAC stiffness or aggregated total symptom scores. The report states there were no major adverse events. Key limitation: the pilot was short, and its benefits, safety, and long-term use could not be confirmed. [68]\n\nPrevention of knee pain or mood effects in active military trainees: Randomized controlled trial; 180 subjects aged 18–40 years; 3 g OptiMSM daily for 8 weeks versus placebo. KOOS and POMS were assessed at 30 and 60 days, and MSM did not significantly improve any of the five KOOS or six POMS subscales. The summary states that 3 g daily can be used safely, but does not provide adverse-event details. Key limitation: no significant preventive or mood benefit was demonstrated during the 8-week intervention. [69]",
