    "evidenceByGoal": "Clinically studied goal: knee osteoarthritis pain and function. Findings are kept specific to the reviewed products rather than transferred across formulations.\n\nHyalgan record (Altman and Moskowitz): design: double-blind, masked-observer, multicenter randomized clinical trial; population: 495 patients with idiopathic knee osteoarthritis; intervention: intra-articular hyaluronic acid, 20 mg per injection, 5 weekly injections; duration: 26 weeks; comparators: placebo and oral naproxen; outcomes: 50-foot-walk pain, WOMAC pain/stiffness/function, and categorical pain. At week 26, HA improved 50-foot-walk pain versus placebo, and WOMAC pain and physical function were significantly better; adverse events: injection-site pain occurred in 23% of HA-treated patients versus 13% of placebo-treated patients, caused withdrawal in 6 patients, and one withdrawal was associated with HA; key limitation: only 67% completed the trial, and the all-randomized 20-mm pain-improvement comparison was not statistically significant. [79]\n\nHyalart record (Miltner et al.): design: prospective controlled right/left comparison; population: 43 patients, 20 males and 23 females, aged 55–78 years, with bilateral knee osteoarthritis of Kellgren stage II–III; intervention: 20 mg hyaluronic acid in 2 mL, 1 injection weekly for 5 weeks; duration: not verified in reviewed source; comparator: contralateral knee; outcomes: isokinetic muscle strength, total work, Lequesne score, and pain VAS, with reported improvements after treatment; adverse events: not verified in reviewed source; key limitation: the reviewed source reports a contralateral-knee control, while placebo masking and other design details were not verified in reviewed source. [80]\n\nHyalgan record (Frizziero et al.): design: pilot, open clinical trial; population: 40 patients with knee osteoarthritis; intervention: 20 mg/2 mL intra-articular HA once weekly for 5 weeks; duration: assessment at 6 months; comparator: baseline, with no separate control group verified in reviewed source; outcomes: microarthroscopic and morphological cartilage/synovial measures, pain, joint mobility, and joint effusion; the source reports that 32.5% improved in cartilage-lesion grading and/or extension, 7.5% worsened, and pain and joint mobility improved; adverse events: the treatment was reported as well tolerated; key limitation: the study was a pilot open trial and the source states that placebo-controlled studies were warranted. [81]",
