    "evidenceByGoal": "Oral antiplaque, plaque formation, and gingival inflammation — Record 1: design: two double-blinded randomized studies using mastic chewing gum; population: systemically and periodontally healthy dental students; sample: 20 students in the saliva-bacteria trial and 10 participants in the 7-day plaque/gingival trial. [82] Dose: not verified in reviewed source. Duration: saliva samples were assessed at 1, 2, 3, and 4 hours, while plaque and gingival inflammation were assessed over 7 days. [82] Comparator: placebo gum. [82] Outcomes: bacterial colonies were significantly reduced during 4 hours of mastic chewing; the mastic group had lower plaque index than placebo (2.69 ± 0.29 vs 3.15 ± 0.24; P = 0.001) and lower gingival index (0.44 ± 0.15 vs 0.66 ± 0.23; P = 0.021). [82] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nOral antiplaque, antigingivitis, and antibacterial activity — Record 2: design: double-blind randomized controlled trial; population: male and female patients aged 21–40 years with chronic generalized gingivitis; sample: 60 patients, with 30 in the mastic chewing-gum group and 30 in the placebo group. [83] Dose: not verified in reviewed source. Duration: measurements were reported at baseline, day 15, and day 21. [83] Comparator: placebo chewing gum. [83] Outcomes: plaque index, gingival index, and mean anaerobic colony-forming units in supragingival plaque were assessed; reductions were reported in both groups and were more marked in the mastic group, with statistically significant between-group results (P < 0.05). [83] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nDiabetic gastroparesis symptoms — Record 3: design: 24-week observational follow-up of a single-center double-blind noninferiority randomized controlled trial; population: patients with diabetic gastroparesis; sample: 38 individuals, equally randomized to two groups. [84] Dose: not verified in reviewed source. Duration: 24 weeks, with comparison to the 2-month postintervention assessment. [84] Comparator: levosulpiride. [84] Outcome: GCSI score; the between-group change at 24 weeks was statistically significant (P < 0.001), while the mastic-gum arm's change from the 2-month postintervention value was nonsignificant (16.7 ± 3.81 vs 16.35 ± 2.27; intragroup P = 0.89). [84] Adverse events: the report states there were no obvious significant adverse effects. [84] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. Evidence remains formulation-specific: the oral studies used mastic chewing gum, whereas Record 3 evaluated a Pistacia lentiscus plant extract described as mastic gum; no cross-formulation efficacy inference is made.",
