    "evidenceByGoal": "Cognition: A two-arm, parallel-group, 6-month randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial enrolled 90 adults aged 40–75 years with self-reported cognitive complaints; participants received 10 mg lutein plus 2 mg zeaxanthin once daily or placebo. Visual episodic memory and visual learning improved versus placebo (p = 0.005 and p = 0.001), while other cognitive and self-report outcomes did not differ significantly. Adverse events were well tolerated with no significant adverse effects reported. Key limitation: the source reports no significant effects on the other assessed cognitive and self-report measures. [85]\n\nNonexudative age-related macular degeneration: The 12-month LUTEGA randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled parallel clinical trial included 172 individuals with nonexudative AMD; 145 completed it. The intervention was a fixed combination of lutein, zeaxanthin, docosahexaenoic acid, and eicosapentaenoic acid at either 10 mg/1 mg/100 mg/30 mg daily or twice that dose, compared with placebo. Plasma xanthophyll concentrations, macular pigment optical density, and plasma antioxidant capacity increased; the source reports that effects appeared after 1 month and remained through the end of the study. Adverse events and a key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. [86]\n\nGlycemic, kidney, liver, and bone-related outcomes: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study enrolled 30 healthy participants aged 35–75 years, assigned to lutein and zeaxanthin or placebo groups. The source reports a 5-week assessment, lower HbA1c in the treatment group, and measurements of eAG, kidney-function markers, liver-function markers, and bone-health-related outcomes. Dose, comparator dosing details, and specific quantitative HbA1c outcome data were not verified in the reviewed source. No severe adverse effects were reported. Key limitation: the record is a medRxiv preprint and the reviewed source does not provide sufficient dose and detailed outcome information for a complete clinical assessment. [87]",
